  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is running hot and holding pace; Hicksville is flagging operationally and structurally.\n\n**Port Washington:** Yesterday was your strongest day in weeks. Six groomers delivered $598 per groomer\u201415% above your trailing 30-day baseline\u2014on 25 core grooms and strong retail (up 20%). You're now +49% year-to-date on revenue and +51% on appointments, which is exceptional. Month-to-date you're tracking dead-on expectation ($41,924 actual vs. $41,863 adjusted), meaning this isn't calendar noise\u2014it's real performance. One thing to watch: bather hours hit zero yesterday (trailing avg 4.5h/day), which suppressed add-on attach slightly (62% vs. 68%). Bring bather coverage back consistently; you have the groomer capacity to support it. Retention is also improving\u2014your 12+ week visit gap is shrinking, a leading indicator that customers are booking tighter cycles.\n\n**Hicksville:** Yesterday's 29% revenue drop is real and concerns me. You had only 2 groomers on the floor producing $442 each (above baseline, so they worked hard), but that's a capacity problem, not effort. Week-to-date you're down 42% on appointments; month-to-date you're still +8% on revenue and +14.7% on adjusted expectation, so July fundamentals are sound\u2014but this week's collapse in bookings is a scheduling or demand signal. Retail also cratered (down 45%), which could indicate front-of-house focus dropping or customer flow issues. The visit-cadence drift is moving the wrong way (growing 12+ week bucket = stealth churn). This location needs immediate attention on booking depth and retail discipline.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today: Port Washington 6 groomers + 7.0h bathers, Hicksville 3 groomers + 0h bathers; next 7 days: Port Washington 34 groomer-days, Hicksville 14 groomer-days."
